class.description:
[XML] converts XML documents to [Dictionary] trees and back, with [XMLElement] views to read and change the tree. Parsing, errors, [method TextDataFormat.load] and [method TextDataFormat.save] come from [TextDataFormat]; [method parse_string] and [method stringify] work on data without an instance, [method serialize] and [method deserialize] on resources.
A document is read into the [Dictionary] of its root element. Every element is a [Dictionary] with these keys:
- [code]"name"[/code]: the tag name ([String]).
- [code]"attributes"[/code]: a [Dictionary] of attribute name to value (both [String]).
- [code]"children"[/code]: an [Array] of child elements ([Dictionary]) and text nodes ([String]), in document order. This keeps mixed content such as [code]<p>Hello <b>big</b> world</p>[/code] in order.
- [code]"text"[/code]: the text of the direct children joined together, for convenience.
[codeblocks]
[gdscript]
var xml = XML.new()
if xml.parse("<window width=\"1280\"><title>Game</title></window>") == OK:
	var root = xml.get_data()
	print(root["attributes"]["width"]) # Prints 1280
	print(root["children"][0]["text"]) # Prints Game
else:
	print(xml.get_error_message()) # For example "Line 2: ..."; the line alone is xml.get_error_line().

var element = { "name": "note", "attributes": { "to": "Ann" }, "text": "Hi" }
print(XML.stringify(element)) # Prints the declaration and <note to="Ann">Hi</note>
[/gdscript]
[csharp]
var xml = new Xml();
if (xml.Parse("<window width=\"1280\"><title>Game</title></window>") == Error.Ok)
{
	var root = xml.GetData();
	GD.Print(root["attributes"].AsGodotDictionary()["width"]); // Prints 1280
	GD.Print(root["children"].AsGodotArray()[0].AsGodotDictionary()["text"]); // Prints Game
}
else
{
	GD.Print(xml.GetErrorMessage()); // For example "Line 2: ..."; the line alone is xml.GetErrorLine().
}

var element = new Godot.Collections.Dictionary
{
	{ "name", "note" },
	{ "attributes", new Godot.Collections.Dictionary { { "to", "Ann" } } },
	{ "text", "Hi" },
};
GD.Print(Xml.Stringify(element));
[/csharp]
[/codeblocks]
[b]Round trip:[/b] reading drops whitespace-only text between elements (indentation), but keeps CDATA sections as text. Writing puts whitespace-only text into CDATA and writes an element that contains text on one line, so no indentation becomes part of the text. Therefore [code]XML.parse_string(XML.stringify(data)) == data[/code] for all data returned by [method parse_string]. Adjacent text nodes are joined into one [String]. Comments, processing instructions and the XML declaration are not kept.
[b]Resource and files:[/b] [XML] is a [Resource] (see [TextDataFormat]). [code]load("res://x.xml")[/code] of a plain file returns an instance, while files with a [code]$type[/code] marker load as the serialized resource. Plain files load with the project default options. The instance methods [method TextDataFormat.load] and [method TextDataFormat.save] work like [method ConfigFile.load] and [method ConfigFile.save]: they do not use the resource cache and do not set [member Resource.resource_path]. In exported games [code]res://[/code] is read-only, so save to [code]user://[/code].
[b]Changes:[/b] changing the data ([member TextDataFormat.data] in the inspector, [member TextDataFormat.data], [XMLElement] writes) drops the kept parsed text, so comments and formatting of the file are lost on the next save. [member TextDataFormat.options] set to [code]null[/code] uses the project settings.
[b]Tree and elements:[/b] [member TextDataFormat.data] is the element [Dictionary] tree, stored once. [method get_root] returns an [XMLElement], a lazy view of the root element that is created on first use; writes through an [XMLElement] change the tree. Paths of [method find_first] and [method find_all] are element names separated by [code]/[/code], relative to the root element ([code]"items/item"[/code]; the root's own name is not part of the path).
[codeblocks]
[gdscript]
var xml = XML.new()
xml.parse("<shop><item id=\"1\">Sword</item><item id=\"2\">Shield</item></shop>")
for item in xml.find_all("item"):
	print(item.get_attribute("id"), " ", item.text) # Prints "1 Sword", then "2 Shield"
xml.find_first("item").text = "Axe" # Changes the tree; comments and formatting are lost on save.
[/gdscript]
[csharp]
var xml = new Xml();
xml.Parse("<shop><item id=\"1\">Sword</item><item id=\"2\">Shield</item></shop>");
foreach (var item in xml.FindAll("item"))
{
	GD.Print(item.GetAttribute("id"), " ", item.Text); // Prints "1 Sword", then "2 Shield"
}
xml.FindFirst("item").Text = "Axe";
[/csharp]
[/codeblocks]
[b]Note:[/b] The whole file is kept in memory as dictionaries. For huge files use [XMLParser] to read and [XMLWriter] to write.
[b]Note:[/b] In C# the class is [code]XmlElement[/code], which clashes with [code]System.Xml.XmlElement[/code] when both namespaces are imported. Use the qualified name [code]Godot.XmlElement[/code].
[b]Note:[/b] The static methods can be called from any thread. An [XML] instance should be used by one thread at a time.
